# Directives here land inside the `reverse_proxy` block, the way tls.d's land
# inside the site block. Empty by default, which is correct for a server that
# visitors reach directly.
#
# WHAT THIS IS FOR: behind a CDN or any other intermediary in front of Caddy --
# Cloudflare is the common one -- the address Caddy observes on the incoming
# connection is the intermediary's, not the visitor's. Caddy will not read a
# forwarded header from a peer it has not been told to trust, and that refusal
# is the right default: any client can send `X-Forwarded-For`, so trusting it
# unconditionally would let a visitor choose their own apparent IP.
#
# `trusted_proxies` is how you name the peers whose forwarded header is
# believable. Drop a file in here:
#
#     trusted_proxies static 173.245.48.0/20 103.21.244.0/22 ...
#
# The ranges are your CDN's published egress list, and they change -- Cloudflare
# publishes theirs at https://www.cloudflare.com/ips/. A stale list does not
# fail loudly: an unlisted range is simply untrusted, and events from visitors
# arriving through it record the CDN's address instead of theirs.
#
# ONLY LIST RANGES YOU ACTUALLY SIT BEHIND. `trusted_proxies static 0.0.0.0/0`
# trusts everyone, which is the same as having no check at all -- it lets any
# client claim any IP by setting a header.
#
# This has no visible effect yet: GeoIP returns an empty country, region and
# city for every event, so nothing currently reads the client address. It
# starts to matter when that changes. Adding it now means the mechanism is
# there, documented, and not discovered later by someone wondering why every
# visitor appears to be in the same datacentre.
#
# This file is not decoration. With an empty directory Caddy logs
# "No files matching import glob pattern" on every start, which reads as a
# misconfiguration to anyone tailing the logs for the first time.
